09:16 < cfields> proposed topic: potential feature-freeze exception for vectorized-chacha20
09:17 < stickies-v> #topic potential feature-freeze exception for vectorized-chacha20 (cfields)
09:17 < cfields> Very sorry for coming down to the wire here, but I'm curious if there's any appetite for an exception to the feature freeze for the vectorized chacha20 impl. It speeds up chacha20 by 1.5-3x (depending on platform/compiler), which is currently one of the most obvious pain points that shows up in flame graphs. I opened the PR months ago but I've been waiting to see what happened with upstream GCC (they're working on a substantial vectorization 
09:17 < cfields> improvement that I assumed existed there while working on the PR. They've responded to my bug reports and I'm confident that the improvements will land, but haven't yet so I've decided to pivot). I'm working on a 128bit version (with l0rinc's help) that should perform better everywhere (though can be replaced with a better 256bit version once GCC gets its act together).
09:18 < cfields> I can commit to spending the next week working on it if there's interest in doing some focused review for v32.
09:18 < cfields> #34083
09:18 < corebot> https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/34083 | Add initial vectorized chacha20 implementation for 2-3x speedup by theuni · Pull Request #34083 · bitcoin/bitcoin · GitHub
09:19 < sipa> next week is a bad time for me, i don't think i can commit to reviewing
09:19 < cfields> Nothing about it is urgent, it'd just be a nice speedup to ship for 32.
09:19 < l0rinc> It's not technically a feature, optimizations can be considered bugfixes - I can continue reviewing
09:19 < stickies-v> this looks like a cool improvement but i think it'd be equally cool to ship in 33?
09:20 -!- viniciuscestarii [~viniciusc@2804:30c:1634:d500:cda9:99be:fc5c:3aac] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
09:21 < cfields> Ok, wfm.
09:21 < l0rinc> I'm fine with both, we have a few other optimizations lined up anyway
09:22 < cfields> my fault for letting it sit there for so long.
09:22 < cfields> thanks. next topic :)
09:22 < stickies-v> so yeah, PSA: today is feature freeze
09:23 < stickies-v> should we talk a bit more about the co-authorship thing? i don't really care much about/for it but it seems to have come up a few times
09:24 < stickies-v> i think one group sees it as a tool to figure out who knows the most about a piece of code/is a person to talk to, another sees it as a way to give credit / say thank you
09:25 < stickies-v> oh and also it was mentioned as a way to express copyright
09:26 < sr_gi> Context?
09:26 < sipa> stickies-v: i was just comparing it with copyright as a bar
09:27 < stickies-v> sr_gi: https://bitcoin-irc.chaincode.com/bitcoin-core-dev/2026-08-18#1247067 (and following days)
09:27 < corebot> stickies-v: Error: That URL raised <HTTP Error 404: Not Found>
09:27 < sr_gi> :tumbsup:
09:27 < stickies-v> sipa: oh sorry, thanks for correcting
09:27 < sipa> i see it as a way of giving credit, and the bar that sounds reasonable for that seems to roughly match the point where someome could claim cooyright
09:27 < l0rinc> copyright is a bad example here: https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35794#issuecomment-5072615311: Relevant U.S. Copyright Office guidance page 5
09:27 < l0rinc> > Applicants should not list an AI technology or the company that provided it as an author or co-author simply because they used it.
09:28 -!- winterrdog [~winterrdo@102.222.234.137] has quit [Read error: Connection reset by peer]
09:29 < stickies-v> i use it when someone has contributed significant amounts of code, or in rare cases even when there was no/little code but a significant amount of architectural/design ideas that made me change course
09:29 < l0rinc> I also see it as giving credit to thank people who helped in shaping the outcome. A good recent example is https://x.com/Rob1Ham/status/2090271687090520435, Rob spent a lot of time and money on finding these, the least we can do is acknowledge his work by adding him as coauthor.
09:29 < sr_gi> I've been using it myself to give credit, specially if someone else has started the work and I've picked it up later, but the base is still theirs (e.g. Gleb with Erlay)
09:29 < sipa> How so? AI tools don't have copyright, and we don't add them as co-author.
09:30 < sipa> So the analogy seems to work.
09:30 -!- winterrdog [~winterrdo@102.222.234.137] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
09:30 < l0rinc> AI is tool, we don't add clang-tidy as coauthor either
