13:45 < afiore> Hi. Regarding the co-author discussion, I feel the need to speak up because of my experience with IP law and the USPTO (I'm not a lawyer).
13:45 < afiore> Re: #35794, "don't have to" doesn't equate to "will be protected in the future for past committed work" - i.e. ex post facto would likely not apply.
13:45 < corebot> https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/35794 | doc: Discourage adding AI agents as commit (co)-authors by sedited · Pull Request #35794 · bitcoin/bitcoin · GitHub
13:45 < afiore> The document that l0rinc cites also states "Based on these developments, the Office concludes that public guidance is needed on the registration of works containing AI-generated content"; i.e., this guidance only applies to *registering* with the copyright the office - not defending unregistered copywritten work now or in the future.
13:46 < afiore> Further more, the way these LLMs are used are not "tools"; they are full-fledged systems of copyright violation concealed amongst layers of relatively good-faith use. For example, there is ongoing litigation that will set industry precedent, once all appeals are exhausted: https://githubcopilotlitigation.com/case-updates.html
13:46 < afiore> In my view, not only should co-authorship be included for future-proofing (it's literally not the commit author's intellectual product), but people should also re-think how they commit atomically so they don't need co-authorship to begin with.
13:46 < afiore> Further more, a friendly "shout-out" or acknowledgement of attribution doesn't equate to co-authorship. It seems, in most cases, people are conflating the two. I think descriptive acknowledgements are great and can be included in the log.
